  "explanation": {
    "how_solved": "When I first sat down with the Easy puzzle, I immediately looked for the 'empty' constraints at [4,0] and [4,2]. Since those spots have to be empty, it limits where the dominoes can sit on the bottom row. I noticed the sum constraint of 6 at [2,0] and [3,0] and looked at my available dominoes. The [0,6] domino fit perfectly there, especially since [1,0] and [2,0] had an 'equals' constraint with another region. Moving to the Medium puzzle, the sum of 4 at [0,1] and [1,1] was my starting point. I tested a few combinations of the smaller dominoes like [0,1] and [2,1] until the grid started to make sense. The hardest part was the Hard puzzle. I saw a huge 'unequal' region covering most of the bottom left. I had to carefully place the [6,3], [5,0], and [2,6] dominoes so that no numbers repeated in that zone. I used the sum of 12 at [2,0] and [3,0] as an anchor, knowing that only a [6,6] or similar high-value domino could get me close, but since I had to work with the specific set provided, I mapped out the [2,6] and [4,0] connections until the whole board clicked into place.",
    "learned": "I learned that paying attention to 'empty' cells is actually more helpful than looking at the sums first. It narrows down the physical space where dominoes can even exist. I also found a cool pattern in the Hard puzzle where the 'unequal' region forced the placement of the [6,3] domino in a very specific orientation because the 6 couldn't touch other 6s already placed in that zone. It's like a mix of Sudoku and a jigsaw puzzle.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What does the 'empty' region type mean?",
        "answer": "It means that specific coordinate on the grid must remain blank; you cannot place any part of a domino on that square."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I handle the 'unequal' constraint in the Hard puzzle?",
        "answer": "This rule means every single number placed within that highlighted region must be different from the others. If there are seven cells in the region, you need to use seven different numbers from 0 to 6."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is there always only one solution?",
        "answer": "Yes, NYT Pips puzzles are designed so that there is only one logically sound way to place all the dominoes while satisfying every region's rule."
      }
